thon3.13/dist-packages (from dask>=0.18.0->linopy>=0.9.0->pypsa) (8.5.0)\n","Requirement already satisfied: cloudpickle>=3.0.0 in /usr/local/lib/python3.13/dist-packages (from dask>=0.18.0->linopy>=0.9.0->pypsa) (3.1.2)\n","Requirement already satisfied: fsspec>=2021.09.0 in /usr/local/lib/python3.13/dist-packages (from dask>=0.18.0->linopy>=0.9.0->pypsa) (2025.12.0)\n","Requirement already satisfied: partd>=1.4.0 in /usr/local/lib/python3.13/dist-packages (from dask>=0.18.0->linopy>=0.9.0->pypsa) (1.4.2)\n","Requirement already satisfied: pyyaml>=5.4.1 in /usr/local/lib/python3.13/dist-packages (from dask>=0.18.0->linopy>=0.9.0->pypsa) (6.0.3)\n","Requirement already satisfied: MarkupSafe>=2.0 in /usr/local/lib/python3.13/dist-packages (from jinja2>=2.10.1->pydeck>=0.6->pypsa) (3.0.3)\n","Requirement already satisfied: polars-runtime-32==1.35.2 in /usr/local/lib/python3.13/dist-packages (from polars>=1.31.1->linopy>=0.9.0->pypsa) (1.35.2)\n","Requirement already satisfied: locket in /usr/local/lib/python3.13/dist-packages (from partd>=1.4.0->dask>=0.18.0->linopy>=0.9.0->pypsa) (1.0.0)\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"lIkeXQUlTKHP"},"source":["## Basic Structure"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"0o-IapsqTKHP"},"source":["| Component | Description |\n","| --- | --- |\n","| [Network](https://docs.pypsa.org/latest/user-guide/design/#network-object) | Container for all components. |\n","| [Bus](https://docs.pypsa.org/latest/user-guide/design/#buses) | Node where components attach. |\n","| [Carrier](https://docs.pypsa.org/latest/user-guide/design/#network-components) | Energy carrier or technology (e.g. electricity, hydrogen, gas, coal, oil, biomass, on-/offshore wind, solar). Can track properties such as specific carbon dioxide emissions or nice names and colors for plots. |\n","| [Load](https://docs.pypsa.org/latest/user-guide/design/#network-components) | Energy consumer (e.g. electricity demand). |\n","| [Generator](https://docs.pypsa.org/latest/user-guide/design/#network-components) | Generator (e.g. power plant, wind turbine, PV panel). |\n","| [Line](https://docs.pypsa.org/latest/user-guide/design/#network-components) | Power distribution and transmission lines (overhead and cables). |\n","| [Link](https://docs.pypsa.org/latest/user-guide/design/#network-components) | Links connect two buses with controllable energy flow, direction-control and losses. They can be used to model: <ul><li>HVDC links</li><li>HVAC lines (neglecting KVL, only net transfer capacities (NTCs))</li><li>conversion between carriers (e.g. electricity to hydrogen in electrolysis)</li></ul> |\n","| [StorageUnit](https://docs.pypsa.org/latest/user-guide/design/#network-components) | Storage with fixed nominal energy-to-power ratio. |\n","| [GlobalConstraint](https://docs.pypsa.org/latest/user-guide/design/#network-components) | Constraints affecting many components at once, such as emission limits. |\n","| [Store](https://docs.pypsa.org/latest/user-guide/design/#network-components) | Storage with separately extendable energy capacity. |\n","|  | **not used in this course** |\n","| [LineType](https://docs.pypsa.org/latest/user-guide/design/#network-components) | Standard line types. |\n","| [Transformer](https://docs.pypsa.org/latest/user-guide/design/#network-components) | 2-winding transformer. |\n","| [TransformerType](https://docs.pypsa.org/latest/user-guide/design/#network-components) | Standard types of 2-winding transformer. |\n","| [ShuntImpedance](https://docs.pypsa.org/latest/user-guide/design/#network-components) | Shunt. |\n","\n","\n",":::{note}\n","Links in the table lead to documentation for each component.\n",":::\n","\n","<img src=\"https://docs.pypsa.org/latest/assets/images/buses.png\" width=\"500px\" />\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"VYCQOkCXTKHP"},"source":[":::{warning}\n","Per unit values of voltage and impedance are used internally for network calculations. It is assumed internally that the base power is **1 MW**.\n",":::\n","\n","## From structured data to optimisation\n","\n","The design principle of PyPSA is that basically each component is associated with a set of variables and constraints that will be added to the optimisation model based on the input data stored for the components.\n","\n","For an *hourly* electricity market simulation, PyPSA will solve an optimisation problem that looks like this\n","\n","\\begin{equation}\n","\\min_{g_{i,s,t}; f_{\\ell,t}; g_{i,r,t,\\text{charge}}; g_{i,r,t,\\text{discharge}}; e_{i,r,t}} \\sum_s o_{s} g_{i,s,t}\n","\\end{equation}\n","such that\n","\\begin{align}\n","0 & \\leq g_{i,s,t} \\leq \\hat{g}_{i,s,t} G_{i,s}  & \\text{generation limits : generator} \\\\\n","-F_\\ell &\\leq f_{\\ell,t} \\leq F_\\ell & \\text{transmission limits : line}  \\\\\n","d_{i,t} &= \\sum_s g_{i,s,t} + \\sum_r g_{i,r,t,\\text{discharge}} - \\sum_r g_{i,r,t,\\text{charge}} - \\sum_\\ell K_{i\\ell} f_{\\ell,t} & \\text{KCL : bus} \\\\\n"," 0 &=\\sum_\\ell C_{\\ell c} x_\\ell f_{\\ell,t} & \\text{KVL : cycles} \\\\\n","0 & \\leq g_{i,r,t,\\text{discharge}} \\leq G_{i,r,\\text{discharge}}& \\text{discharge limits : storage unit} \\\\\n","    0 & \\leq g_{i,r,t,\\text{charge}} \\leq G_{i,r,\\text{charge}} & \\text{charge limits : storage unit} \\\\\n","    0 & \\leq e_{i,r,t} \\leq E_{i,r} & \\text{energy limits : storage unit} \\\\\n","    e_{i,r,t} &= \\eta^0_{i,r,t} e_{i,r,t-1} + \\eta^1_{i,r,t}g_{i,r,t,\\text{charge}} -  \\frac{1}{\\eta^2_{i,r,t}} g_{i,r,t,\\text{discharge}} & \\text{consistency : storage unit} \\\\\n","    e_{i,r,0} & = e_{i,r,|T|-1}  & \\text{cyclicity : storage unit}\n","\\end{align}\n","\n","**Decision variables:**\n","\n","- $g_{i,s,t}$ is the generator dispatch at bus $i$, technology $s$, time step $t$,\n","- $f_{\\ell,t}$ is the power flow in line $\\ell$,\n","- $g_{i,r,t,\\text{dis-/charge}}$ denotes the charge and discharge of storage unit $r$ at bus $i$ and time step $t$,\n","- $e_{i,r,t}$ is the state of charge of storage $r$ at bus $i$ and time step $t$.\n","\n","**Parameters:**\n","\n","- $o_{i,s}$ is the marginal generation cost of technology $s$ at bus $i$,\n","- $x_\\ell$ is the reactance of transmission line $\\ell$,\n","- $K_{i\\ell}$ is the incidence matrix,\n","- $C_{\\ell c}$ is the cycle matrix,\n","- $G_{i,s}$ is the nominal capacity of the generator of technology $s$ at bus $i$,\n","- $F_{\\ell}$ is the rating of the transmission line $\\ell$,\n","- $E_{i,r}$ is the energy capacity of storage $r$ at bus $i$,\n","- $\\eta^{0/1/2}_{i,r,t}$ denote the standing (0), charging (1), and discharging (2) efficiencies.\n","\n",":::{note}\n","For a full reference to the optimisation problem description, see https://pypsa.readthedocs.io/en/latest/optimal_power_flow.html\n",":::"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"8ZBPIuvgTKHR"},"source":["## Simple electricity market example"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"-C1UEj_vTKHR"},"source":["Let's get acquainted with PyPSA to build a variant of one of the simple electricity market models we previously built in `linopy`. Hopefully, it can convince you that it will be easier to work with PyPSA than with `linopy`.\n","\n","We have the following data:"]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["import pandas as pd\n","import numpy as np\n","\n","# Obtained from IEMOP Data from 2023\n","filename = \"/content/PyPSA_Dataset_Bohol.xlsx\"\n","buses_df = pd.read_excel(filename, sheet_name=\"buses\")\n","loads_df = pd.read_excel(filename, sheet_name=\"loads\")\n","carriers_df = pd.read_excel(filename, sheet_name=\"carriers\")\n","generators_df = pd.read_excel(filename, sheet_name=\"generators\")\n","lines_df = pd.read_excel(filename, sheet_name=\"lines\")\n","\n","# Obtain from Renewable Energy Ninja: https://www.renewables.ninja/; Assumed same weather data for 2023\n","loads_t_df = pd.read_excel(filename, sheet_name=\"loads_t\", index_col=0, parse_dates=True, date_format='%m/%d/%y %H:%M')\n","generators_t_df = pd.read_excel(filename, sheet_name=\"generators_t\", index_col=0, parse_dates=True, date_format='%m/%d/%y %H:%M')\n","loads_t_df.index = pd.to_datetime(loads_t_df.index)\n","generators_t_df.index = pd.to_datetime(generators_t_df.index)"],"metadata":{"id":"v324H2Ul2njO","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1791450742370,"user_tz":-120,"elapsed":1191,"user":{"displayName":"Ariz Salac","userId":"14132994972447731579"}}},"execution_count":3,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"5ciBLfSNTKHT"},"source":["## Building a basic network"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["We now add our data from our spreadsheet file. Make sure to upload the file on the directory."],"metadata":{"id":"RqCnbhNg289C"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"K4zhMJtsTKHT"},"source":["By convention, PyPSA is imported without an alias:"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":4,"metadata":{"tags":[],"id":"5ggXYKOKTKHT","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1791450767945,"user_tz":-120,"elapsed":6120,"user":{"displayName":"Ariz Salac","userId":"14132994972447731579"}}},"outputs":[],"source":["import pypsa"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"hNNMyXGCTKHT"},"source":["First, we create a new network object which serves as the overall container for all components."]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["n = pypsa.Network()"],"metadata":{"id":"rsrF7AKIqdRL","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1791450768214,"user_tz":-120,"elapsed":241,"user":{"displayName":"Ariz Salac","userId":"14132994972447731579"}},"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"outputId":"e10a0234-9088-416e-b426-49e486b4f21a"},"execution_count":5,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stderr","text":["/usr/local/lib/python3.13/dist-packages/pypsa/network/io.py:2082: FutureWarning:\n","\n","pandas infers the `str` dtype for string data since its version 3.0. PyPSA still converts it back to numpy object dtype on import, but will keep it from PyPSA 2.0 on. Set `pypsa.options.api.legacy_string_dtype`